Inputs
------
mu0   = 4*pi*1e-7      = 1.256637061436e-06 H/m
gauss = 1e-4 T, cm = 1e-2 m, erg = 1e-7 J

Flux density B, and polarization J
----------------------------------
1 T   = 1/1e-4                         = 10000 G
1 mT  = 1e-3 T                         = 10 G
1 G   = 1e-4 T                         = 0.1 mT

Field strength H
----------------
1 Oe  = (1 G)/mu0 = 1e-4/(4*pi*1e-7)   = 79.5774715459 A/m
      = 1000/(4*pi) exactly            = 79.5774715459 A/m
1 A/m = mu0/(1 G)                      = 1.2566370614e-02 Oe
      = 4*pi*1e-3 exactly              = 1.2566370614e-02 Oe
1 kA/m= 1e3 A/m                        = 12.566371 Oe

Magnetization M, same units as H
--------------------------------
1 emu (moment) = 1 erg/G               = 0.001 A m^2
1 A m^2                                = 1000 emu
1 emu/cm^3                             = 1000 A/m
1 emu/cm^3, written CGS style as 4*pi*M= 12.566371 G

Magnetic flux
-------------
1 Mx  = 1 G cm^2                       = 1e-08 Wb
1 Wb                                   = 1e+08 Mx

Energy product BH
-----------------
1 G Oe                                 = 0.0079577472 J/m^3
1 MGOe = 1e6 G Oe                      = 7.9577 kJ/m^3
1 kJ/m^3                               = 0.125664 MGOe

Demagnetizing factor, the trap
------------------------------
SI:  H_d = -N M          with  sum(N_x,N_y,N_z) = 1
CGS: H_d = -N' M         with  sum(N'x,N'y,N'z) = 4*pi = 12.566371
N' = 4*pi*N, so a factor of 12.566371 separates two numbers both called N.

Round trip check
----------------
Br = 1.320 T -> 13200 G -> 1.320000 T
HcJ = 875.0 kA/m -> 10995.57 Oe -> 875.000000 kA/m
BHmax = 330.0 kJ/m^3 -> 41.4690 MGOe -> 330.000000 kJ/m^3

The three values above are round trip test inputs, not a real part.
The reference part is not chosen yet. See shared/reference-part.md.

Note on mu0
-----------
Before 2019, mu0 was exactly 4*pi*1e-7 H/m by definition of the ampere.
The 2019 SI redefinition made mu0 an experimentally determined quantity.
It is no longer exact, and the current value must be read from CODATA
rather than assumed. The offset is far below anything this bench can
resolve: a Hall probe of 0.1 percent class accuracy is many orders of
magnitude coarser. Use 4*pi*1e-7 throughout. Do not quote CODATA digits
in any class until the NIST page has actually been fetched.
